# 02 · Essential Eight Gap Assessment (Maturity Level 1): Northside Allied Health

> **Practice exercise. Fictional organisation.** All names, systems and findings are invented. Based on the ASD Essential Eight Maturity Model. Check the latest version on cyber.gov.au before reusing.

[← Back to portfolio](../README.md)

## Scope & approach

- **Target:** Maturity Level 1 (ML1), the right starting point for a small clinic facing opportunistic, commodity attacks
- **In scope:** 22 Windows PCs, 3 laptops, Microsoft 365 tenant, cloud practice management system (PMS)
- **Method:** (simulated) interview with the Practice Manager and the managed IT provider, review of M365 admin settings, and a sample check of 5 PCs
- **Status key:** ✅ Met · 🟡 Partially met · ❌ Not met

## Summary

| Strategy | Status | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Patch applications | 🟡 Partial | High |
| 2. Patch operating systems | 🟡 Partial | High |
| 3. Multi-factor authentication | ❌ Not met | **Critical** |
| 4. Restrict administrative privileges | ❌ Not met | High |
| 5. Application control | ❌ Not met | Medium |
| 6. Restrict Microsoft Office macros | ✅ Met | — |
| 7. User application hardening | 🟡 Partial | Medium |
| 8. Regular backups | ❌ Not met | **Critical** |

**Overall: Maturity Level 0.** One strategy meets ML1 and three partly meet it. Fixing MFA and backups first would remove the two most serious exposures.

## Detailed assessment

### 3. Multi-factor authentication ❌ (Critical)
| | |
|---|---|
| **ML1 intent** | Stop stolen passwords alone from giving access to internet-facing services |
| **Evidence expected** | MFA enforced (e.g. Conditional Access or Security Defaults) for all M365 users; MFA on the PMS |
| **Observed gap** | Security Defaults turned off; 4 of 25 users have enrolled MFA voluntarily; the PMS supports MFA but it isn't enabled |
| **Recommendation** | Turn on Security Defaults (or a Conditional Access policy) for all users; enable MFA in the PMS; brief staff before the rollout |
| **Linked risk** | R1, R3 |

### 8. Regular backups ❌ (Critical)
| | |
|---|---|
| **ML1 intent** | Important data can be recovered after ransomware, deletion or failure |
| **Evidence expected** | Backups done and kept to a defined schedule; restores tested; unprivileged accounts can't change or delete backups |
| **Observed gap** | Monthly manual copy to one USB drive at reception; never test-restored; M365 data not backed up separately |
| **Recommendation** | Automated daily backup of M365 and file data; a copy kept offline or immutable; documented quarterly test restores |
| **Linked risk** | R2, R5. See [audit finding 04](04-audit-finding.md) |

### 4. Restrict administrative privileges ❌ (High)
| | |
|---|---|
| **ML1 intent** | Limit what an attacker can do if a user account is compromised |
| **Evidence expected** | Staff use standard accounts day to day; admin rights only for staff who need them, through separate admin accounts |
| **Observed gap** | All 5 sampled PCs had the daily user as local administrator; the Practice Manager's everyday M365 account is a Global Admin |
| **Recommendation** | Remove local admin rights from staff; create separate admin accounts used only for admin tasks |
| **Linked risk** | R2, R3 |

### 1 & 2. Patch applications / operating systems 🟡 (High)
| | |
|---|---|
| **ML1 intent** | Close known vulnerabilities quickly, especially on internet-facing systems |
| **Evidence expected** | Patches for internet-facing services and common applications applied within 2 weeks (48 hours where an exploit exists); unsupported software removed |
| **Observed gap** | Windows Update is on, but staff often defer restarts. 2 PCs were 3 months behind. One reception PC runs an unsupported PDF reader |
| **Recommendation** | Have the MSP manage patching centrally with forced restarts out of hours; remove unsupported software; report patch status monthly |
| **Linked risk** | R2 |

### 5. Application control ❌ (Medium)
| | |
|---|---|
| **Observed gap** | No application control; users can run any executable from Downloads |
| **Recommendation** | Start with reception and shared PCs: allow only approved applications (e.g. WDAC or AppLocker, managed by the MSP) |

### 7. User application hardening 🟡 (Medium)
| | |
|---|---|
| **Observed gap** | Browsers up to date, but Internet Explorer 11 is still turned on and browser security settings aren't centrally managed |
| **Recommendation** | Turn off IE11; apply baseline browser settings through Intune |

### 6. Restrict Microsoft Office macros ✅
Macros from the internet are blocked by M365 defaults, and no business process needs macros. **No action. Keep monitoring.**

## Suggested roadmap

| Timeframe | Actions |
|---|---|
| **Next 2 weeks** | MFA for M365 and the PMS · start automated backups |
| **Within 30 days** | Remove local admin rights · managed patching · first test restore |
| **Within 90 days** | Application control on shared PCs · browser hardening · reassess against ML1 |

# 01 · Risk Register: Northside Allied Health

> **Practice exercise. Fictional organisation.** All names, systems and figures are invented.

[← Back to portfolio](../README.md)

## Context

| Item | Detail |
|---|---|
| Organisation | Northside Allied Health (fictional), 25 staff, 2 Melbourne clinics |
| Key information assets | ~8,000 patient records (health information) in a cloud practice management system (PMS); Microsoft 365 email and SharePoint; card payments through an integrated terminal |
| Legal / regulatory | Privacy Act 1988 (health information is *sensitive information*), Notifiable Data Breaches (NDB) scheme, Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) |
| Risk owner (overall) | Practice Manager |
| Assessment date | Practice exercise, 2026 |

## Scoring method

**Risk rating = Likelihood (1–5) × Consequence (1–5)**

| Score | Rating | Expected response |
|---|---|---|
| 15–25 | **Extreme** | Treat immediately; report to owners |
| 10–14 | **High** | Treat within 30 days |
| 5–9 | **Medium** | Treat within 90 days |
| 1–4 | **Low** | Accept and monitor |

Likelihood: 1 Rare · 2 Unlikely · 3 Possible · 4 Likely · 5 Almost certain
Consequence: 1 Insignificant · 2 Minor · 3 Moderate · 4 Major · 5 Severe (e.g. notifiable breach of health data, clinics unable to operate)

## Register

| ID | Risk | NIST CSF | Existing controls | L | C | Inherent | Recommended treatment | Owner | Target (residual) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| R1 | **Phishing leads to compromised M365 account**: an attacker reads patient emails and uses the account to send invoice fraud | PR.AA, PR.AT | Default spam filtering; no phishing training | 4 | 4 | **16 Extreme** | Enforce MFA for all M365 accounts; quarterly phishing awareness session; turn on external-sender tagging | Practice Manager | L2 × C4 = 8 Medium |
| R2 | **Ransomware encrypts clinic PCs and file shares**, stopping bookings and billing | PR.PS, RC.RP | Consumer antivirus on PCs; patches applied "when prompted" | 3 | 5 | **15 Extreme** | Centrally managed patching (Essential Eight ML1); application control on reception PCs; tested offline backups (see R5) | IT Provider (MSP) | L2 × C4 = 8 Medium |
| R3 | **Unauthorised access to patient records** through shared or leftover PMS logins (e.g. former staff) | PR.AA | Individual logins exist, but reception shares one account; no offboarding checklist | 3 | 4 | **12 High** | Remove shared accounts; offboarding checklist with same-day access removal; quarterly user access review | Practice Manager | L1 × C4 = 4 Low |
| R4 | **Lost or stolen laptop** used for home telehealth exposes patient data | PR.DS | Windows password only | 3 | 4 | **12 High** | BitLocker full-disk encryption; Intune remote wipe; screen-lock policy | IT Provider (MSP) | L3 × C2 = 6 Medium |
| R5 | **Backups fail when needed**: SharePoint and local files are not recoverable after an incident | RC.RP, PR.DS | Monthly backup to a USB drive kept at reception; never test-restored | 3 | 4 | **12 High** | Automated daily backups with one copy offline/immutable; quarterly test restore with results recorded (see [finding 04](04-audit-finding.md)) | IT Provider (MSP) | L1 × C3 = 3 Low |

## Heat map (inherent risk)

| Likelihood ↓ / Consequence → | 1 Insignificant | 2 Minor | 3 Moderate | 4 Major | 5 Severe |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **5 Almost certain** | | | | | |
| **4 Likely** | | | | 🟥 R1 | |
| **3 Possible** | | | | 🟧 R3, R4, R5 | 🟥 R2 |
| **2 Unlikely** | | | | | |
| **1 Rare** | | | | | |

🟥 Extreme · 🟧 High

## Key takeaways for the Practice Manager

1. **Turn on MFA this week.** It's the cheapest, fastest control and cuts the top-rated risk (R1) the most.
2. **R2 and R5 are linked.** Ransomware becomes survivable once backups are automated, kept partly offline, and *proven* to restore.
3. **Health information is sensitive under the Privacy Act.** A breach affecting patients is likely to be notifiable to the OAIC and to patients, so access control (R3) and encryption (R4) also protect the clinic's legal position.
4. Review this register **every quarter** and after any incident or major system change.

# 04 · Audit-Style Finding: Backups Not Tested

> **Practice exercise. Fictional organisation.** All details are invented.

[← Back to portfolio](../README.md)

| Field | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Finding ID** | NAH-2026-01 |
| **Title** | Backups are taken but never tested for restoration |
| **Rating** | 🟧 **High** |
| **Control area** | Essential Eight: Regular backups · NIST CSF 2.0: RC.RP (Incident Recovery Plan Execution), PR.DS-11 (Backups) |
| **Owner** | IT Provider (MSP), with Practice Manager oversight |

## Condition (what we found)

Practice files and exported PMS reports are copied by hand to a single USB hard drive about once a month. The drive is kept in an unlocked drawer at the main clinic's reception. Staff could not remember any attempt to restore data from it, and there are no records of a test restore. Microsoft 365 data (email, SharePoint, OneDrive) isn't backed up separately.

## Criteria (what should be happening)

- **ASD Essential Eight, ML1:** backups of important data are done and kept according to business continuity requirements, and **restoration is tested** as part of disaster recovery exercises.
- **Privacy Act 1988, APP 11:** take reasonable steps to protect personal information from misuse, interference and loss.
- **Good practice (3-2-1):** three copies of data, on two types of media, with one copy offsite or offline.

## Cause (why it happened)

- No one has formally been made responsible for backups.
- The backup process was set up in 2020 and hasn't been reviewed since.
- People assumed "we have a backup" meant "we can recover".

## Risk (why it matters)

If ransomware, hardware failure or accidental deletion hits the clinic, it may find out *during the incident* that its backups are incomplete, damaged or up to 30 days old. The likely result is days of disruption to bookings and billing, permanent loss of clinical notes, and a possible notifiable data breach if patient records can't be accessed or restored. Having a single drive in a public area also means it could be stolen or damaged.

**Linked risks:** R2 (Ransomware) and R5 (Backup failure) in the [Risk Register](01-risk-register.md).

## Recommendation

1. **Within 2 weeks:** formally make the MSP responsible for backups, with the Practice Manager as risk owner.
2. **Within 30 days:** set up **automated daily backups** of file data and M365, keeping **one copy offline or immutable**.
3. **Within 30 days:** carry out and **document a first test restore** (sample files, a mailbox and a PMS export).
4. **Ongoing:** test restores **every quarter**, record the results, and report any failures to the Practice Manager.
5. Move the USB drive to a locked location while the old process is phased out.

## Management response (example)

> *"Agreed. The MSP will start automated M365 and file backups by 15 December, with the first documented test restore by 31 December. The Practice Manager will review results quarterly."*

# 03 · Privacy Policy Review: Northside Allied Health

> **Practice exercise. Fictional organisation.** The policy reviewed is an invented example. This is a learning exercise, not legal advice.

[← Back to portfolio](../README.md)

## Document reviewed

| Item | Detail |
|---|---|
| Document | *Northside Allied Health Privacy Policy* (fictional), v1.2 |
| Last reviewed | March 2021 (review was due March 2023) |
| Reviewed against | Privacy Act 1988 (Cth): Australian Privacy Principles (APPs) 1, 5, 6, 11, 12 and 13; Notifiable Data Breaches (NDB) scheme; OAIC guidance for health service providers |

## Strengths

- **Plain language.** Patients can read and understand it without legal training.
- **Clear purposes of collection.** It explains why health information is collected (treatment, billing, referrals), in line with APP 1 and APP 5.
- **Access and correction explained.** It tells patients how to request their records (APP 12 and 13).
- **Publicly available** on the clinic's website, as APP 1.4 expects.

## Gaps identified

| # | Gap | Why it matters | Reference |
|---|---|---|---|
| G1 | **No named privacy contact.** The policy says "contact the clinic" with no role or contact details | Patients and the OAIC need a clear point of contact for questions and complaints | APP 1.4(e) |
| G2 | **Vague security wording.** It says only that information is "stored securely" | Gives patients no real assurance and staff no standard to follow; APP 11 requires *reasonable steps* | APP 11.1 |
| G3 | **No retention or destruction rules** | Records are kept forever by default, increasing the impact of any breach; Victorian health records rules set minimum retention periods | APP 11.2; Health Records Act 2001 (Vic) |
| G4 | **No data breach section** | The NDB scheme requires assessing a suspected breach within 30 days and notifying the OAIC and affected individuals if it is an *eligible data breach* | Privacy Act Part IIIC |
| G5 | **Overseas disclosure not addressed.** The cloud PMS may store data offshore | APP 1.4(f)–(g) requires stating whether information is likely to be disclosed overseas, and to which countries | APP 1.4, APP 8 |
| G6 | **Review overdue by more than 3 years** | An out-of-date policy may not reflect current systems (e.g. telehealth, the cloud PMS) | Good governance practice |

## Recommended improvements

1. **Name a Privacy Officer** (e.g. the Practice Manager) with a direct email and phone number. *(G1)*
2. **Describe security safeguards in plain terms**, such as MFA, encryption, role-based access and staff training, and link to an internal Information Security Policy. *(G2)*
3. **Add a retention and destruction schedule** that meets Victorian health records requirements, including secure disposal methods. *(G3)*
4. **Add a data breach response section** that explains how breaches are assessed and when patients and the OAIC will be notified, and link it to an internal Data Breach Response Plan. *(G4)*
5. **Confirm where the PMS vendor stores data** and state any overseas disclosure. *(G5)*
6. **Set an annual review date** and a trigger to review the policy after any new system or significant incident. *(G6)*

## Priority

| Priority | Items |
|---|---|
| High | G4 (breach response), G5 (overseas disclosure) |
| Medium | G1, G2, G3 |
| Low | G6 (fixed as part of this update) |
